<!--
  White.

  It has been the app's blue, then near-black. Blue read as one more blue tile among a
  dozen. Near-black was the app's own ground, which was the right instinct for matching
  the app and the wrong one for a home screen: a dark disc on a dark wallpaper loses its
  edge, and the mask's shape is part of how an icon is recognised at a glance.

  White is what most of the icons beside it already do — of the drawer's neighbours,
  Authenticator, YouTube, Calendar, Contacts, Camera, Assistant, Phone and Messages all
  sit on a white bleed. It is the background that disappears, which is the point: the
  handshake is the mark, not the disc it sits on.

  Worth knowing before changing the artwork: the light hand is #FFCDBE, which against
  white is 1.43:1 — it carries on hue, not on lightness. Recolour the hands and the pale
  one is the first thing to check, because contrast maths will call it invisible and the
  eye will not. Against the old near-black it was 13.4:1 and this could not have gone
  wrong.

  Pure #FFF rather than an off-white. The neighbours are pure white, and a near-white
  reads as a slightly grubby version of them rather than as its own choice.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">
    <path
        android:pathData="M0,0h108v108h-108z"
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFFFF" />
</vector>
